.TH SDE-ACCOUNT-RENAME 8 "October 2026" "SDE" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sde-account-rename \- rename user accounts at the next start of the computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/libexec/sde/sde-account-rename
\fBschedule\fR|\fBschedule-self\fR \fB--uid\fR \fIUID\fR \fB--old\fR \fINAME\fR \fB--new\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB--link\fR]
.br
.B /usr/libexec/sde/sde-account-rename
\fBcancel\fR|\fBcancel-self\fR \fB--uid\fR \fIUID\fR
.br
.B /usr/libexec/sde/sde-account-rename
\fBapply\fR | \fBstatus\fR | \fB--version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsde-account-rename\fR renames a user account: its login name, its own
group and, when it is named after the account, its home folder. The Control
Center (Users & Avatar, \fBRename...\fR) runs it through \fBpkexec\fR(1) to
record a request; \fBsde-account-rename.service\fR makes the rename early at
the next boot, before the display manager, logins, logind, AccountsService
and cron start, because a login name cannot change while the person is
logged in.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B schedule-self, schedule
Checks the request as root and writes it to
\fI/var/lib/sde/account-renames/UID.rename\fR. The account \fIUID\fR must
be a person's (UID_MIN to UID_MAX of \fI/etc/login.defs\fR) and be called
\fB--old\fR now; the new name must be a valid login name (a lowercase
letter, then lowercase letters, digits, - and _, at most 32 characters, no
name of the system), not the name of a user or group, not asked for by
another request, and not named by a file that grants something to users
(group member lists, sudo rules, polkit rules, sshd's AllowUsers,
DenyUsers and Match, access.conf, cron.allow, at.allow). \fB--link\fR also
leaves a link from the old home folder. \fBschedule-self\fR is for one's own
account (polkit action org.debion.sde.account-rename.schedule-self, the
user's own password); \fBschedule\fR for any (org.debion.sde.account-rename.schedule,
an administrator's).
.TP
.B cancel-self, cancel
Removes the request (or a failed one) of \fIUID\fR.
.TP
.B apply
Makes the renames (run by the boot service as root; refused through
pkexec). For each request: when programs of the account run, it waits for
the next start. Otherwise each step is written to a journal first: the
account database (\fI/etc/passwd\fR, \fIshadow\fR, \fIgroup\fR,
\fIgshadow\fR, \fIsubuid\fR, \fIsubgid\fR) is saved; \fBusermod -l NEW -d
HOME -m OLD\fR; \fBgroupmod -n NEW OLD\fR for the account's own group;
AccountsService's user file and picture; automatic login of LightDM
(\fIautologin-user\fR, the greeters' \fIlast-user\fR) and GDM
(\fIAutomaticLogin\fR, \fITimedLogin\fR);
the crontab and the mail spool; \fI/etc/subuid\fR and \fI/etc/subgid\fR;
the drop-ins of \fI/etc/sudoers.d\fR (checked with \fBvisudo -c\fR); the
linger flag of systemd; the link. When a step fails, the steps made are
undone in reverse order and the request is marked failed with the reason;
a journal left by a computer that stopped during a rename is undone at the
next start.
.TP
.B status
Prints the requests.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/sde/account-renames/
The requests (UID.rename), journals and saved files of a rename in progress.
.TP
.I /var/log/sde-account-rename.log
What was scheduled, cancelled, renamed, undone or refused (also in the
authpriv syslog).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/sde-account-rename.service
The boot service.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 done, 1 refused or failed (an "ERROR CODE text" line says why), 2 usage.
.SH NOTES
\fI/etc/sudoers\fR itself, sshd's configuration and polkit rules are not
changed; the log lists those that still name the old account. Programs
that stored the old home folder path in their settings may need the link.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBusermod\fR(8), \fBgroupmod\fR(8), \fBpkexec\fR(1),
\fI/usr/share/doc/sde/CONTROL-CENTER.md\fR
